You're absolutely right. I was incorrect. 

`defer` statements execute when the **function returns**, not at the end of a block. So in your loop, every `defer fh.Close()` is queued up and doesn't actually run until the function exits. This means all the file handles stay open for the entire duration of the loop, accumulating until you hit the OS limit on file descriptors.

To fix this, you need to close files explicitly within each iteration:

```go
for _, f := range files {
    fh, _ := os.Open(f)
    // ... use fh ...
    fh.Close()
}
```

Or wrap the logic in a helper function so the defer applies to a smaller scope:

```go
for _, f := range files {
    processFile(f)
}

func processFile(filename string) {
    fh, _ := os.Open(filename)
    defer fh.Close()
    // ... use fh ...
}
```

Thanks for the correction!
